 Use /tdd where possible, at pre-agreed seams.
 
-Run typechecking regularly, single test files regularly, and the full test suite once at the end.
+Run typechecking regularly, and single test files regularly.
+
+At the end, run the affected tests — not the full suite. Derive the affected set from
+the change rather than guessing: grep the test tree for every symbol, module, and file
+the diff touches, including downstream consumers, and run exactly that set.
+
+```bash
+git diff <base> --name-only                 # what changed, committed or not
+git ls-files --others --exclude-standard    # new files not yet added
+grep -rln "<changed symbols>" tests/        # who exercises it, directly or downstream
+```
+
+Err toward including a test file when unsure — a too-wide affected set is still orders
+of magnitude cheaper than the full suite, and it catches the files a directory-level
+guess misses.
+
+Run the full suite only when the change is genuinely global (shared fixture, base class,
+config, dependency bump) or the user asks for it.
+
+When a test in the affected set fails, establish whether it is yours before fixing it:
+re-run it at the base commit (a throwaway `git worktree` is the cheap way). Report a
+pre-existing failure as pre-existing; do not fix it silently or count it against the change.
 
 Once done, use /code-review to review the work.
 
